WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:06.380
This is a pile of paper slips. In about a minute it's going to write a sentence that nobody has ever written.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:16.420
Every slip is a pair of words from a book: the word in the box, and the word that came next. A pair the book says six times gets six slips.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:27.720
Choose a slip to start from. A story starts after a full stop, so take one with a full stop in its box. This one says "it". Write it down.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:41.720
Now find every slip with "it" in its box. There are ten. Three say "sits", three "watches", three "goes", and one says "is". Pick one without choosing: "sits".

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:48.940
Every "sits" slip says "on", and every "on" slip says "the". No choice at all.

00:00:49.450 --> 00:01:00.560
"The" has nineteen slips. Pick one: "dog". And "dog" has four: two full stops, two "runs". Pick one: full stop.

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:13.040
"It sits on the dog." The book never says that. It says the magpie sits on the fence. Nobody wrote this sentence; the slips did, one pair at a time, like dominoes.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:16.940
And here's everything this pile could have written instead.

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:25.520
Every pick sends you down a different path. So chain ten thousand words, cut them back into slips, and sort them.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:38.140
Every chain different. Every chain sounding like the book. That's what Claude or ChatGPT is doing every time it writes a word, with vastly more than a pile of slips.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:51.980
Spread your cutouts out. Write down a starting word, find a slip with that word in its box, and write down its next word. Then find a slip that starts with that. The colours help you find the matches fast.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.340
Start chaining.
